I started making N Body sims after watching Deadlock's video [https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=nZHjD3cI-EU] on the Barnes Hut Algorithm. 
I started with pure python making an object-based tree that used recursive division to be created. 
It was incredibly slow and had many artifacts due to the high theta I had to use to get any reasonable performance. 
Then, after discovering Taichi, I decided to take a more parallel approach. 
A lot of the later work on the sim was created in my AP Computer Science Principles class for the AP performance task. 
I started by making a binary radix tree as that’s friendly to both storage and speed. 
Binary radix trees can be constructed completely in parallel and always have exactly 2N-1 nodes which was very nice since Taichi dynamically sized fields are extremely slow. 
At first when I realized I needed sorted Morton codes I tried making my very own radix sort, but upon failing multiple times I ended up using Taichi’s parallel_sort(). 
From there it was relatively easy to compute forces. After creating the physics, I then created a density based visualizer. 
I did this by adding a number to the pixel (and the surrounding pixels) a particle occupies then applying a function to get the brightness and color. 
At the time I was quite happy with it running 100,000 particles at roughly 20fps. This was around the time I submitted it to the AP Performance Task. 
I then took a break from N-body to work on RingSimV2 for my AP CSP Game Jam. Only recently, in late July, I returned unsatisfied by the performance. 
The motivation for this was mostly due to Deadlock’s video where they made a simulation that ran with millions of particles.
I ran some tests and added Taichi’s built in function profiler and found that the sort I was using to sort the Morton codes was taking hundreds of milliseconds to sort at higher N. 
When I looked into it, I saw that the sort I was using used a modified even-odd sort with 190 serial stages per sort. This was extremely slow, so I returned to making a radix sort.
When I ran some tests on it, it showed ~26ms sort time for N=1,000,000! I plugged it into the sim and after a couple hiccups), it ran like a charm. 
I was now running 500,000 particles at a very acceptable framerate. But with how well my program scaled I thought that I could go higher. But upon trying N=1,000,000, it seemed gravity just turned off. 
There were no errors, no NaNs I could see, and no problems I could find with my force or tree functions. So, I let it be for a while. 
When I showed my family my new snazzy simulation, my sister asked how much I could do. When I told her something broke at 1,000,000, she suggested we find exactly what N it would break. 
We did a bit of a manual binary search and found that at exactly N=809,551 the sim broke. 
Newly motivated to solve this, I did a lot of digging and I found a NaN in the root node’s mass, and upon looking at its children, found that one of them contained a NaN’d position that broke the tree creation. 
After a lot of searching, I figured out what had happened. 
Taichi’s random() function is deterministic, and since I used that for initializing positions, any given particle would be the exact same position every time you ran a sim with the same parameters. 
Turns out at exactly N=809,551 the random() function gives a 0 for both x and y in the positions. A faulty While loop check allowed this to pass through and suddenly the particle was 0 units from the center. 
When it tried to find the velocity, it divides by r and dividing by 0 gave a NaN. 
This then infected the tree and when a particle tried to see if a node was close enough, nothing ever returned True because the node Center Of Mass (COM) positions were always a NaN. 
So, in effect, it turned gravity off. 
Another bug then presented itself when I updated the initializer to give galaxies their own speeds. 
Their cores were being nailed to a spot! When I looked into it further, I realized the mistake was that I did a position half step for the Verlet integration after the tree was built. 
This allowed particles and even galaxy cores to gravitate towards their past selves, and with a dense enough region its past self’s gravity overpowered the gravity of anything else. 
All it took was moving the first position half step to before tree creation and galaxies could move. 
After all that work, I broke the 1,000,000 particle barrier. It runs ~15fps with N=2,000,000 and the frame time scales roughly O(nlogn) as expected [see https://www.desmos.com/calculator/xfcfxqrpbl]. 
I’ve learned a lot from this program including how to make parallel functions and algorithms, work with the GPU, and even some behaviors of 2d gravity simulations.
But I think, at least for now, I have finished this program.
